# What belongs in git here, and what does not.
#
# The skeleton was committed before this file existed, so the README.md and
# .gitkeep files inside the ignored directories below are already tracked and
# survive a clone. .gitignore only ever affects files git is not yet tracking.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- disposable

# Indexes, measurements, staging, batch journals. Rebuilt on demand; deleting
# it costs time on the next run and nothing else.
/cache/

# The built site. Generated by `hypnotica build` and never edited by hand.
/www/*
!/www/README.md


# ------------------------------------------------------------------- payload

# Audio and artwork. A few thousand recordings is a few hundred gigabytes, git
# stores every version of every binary forever and cannot delta them, and no
# forge will take it. Use git-annex, Git LFS, or a backup tool that is not git
# at all — media/README.md weighs those up.
/media/*/*
!/media/*/README.md


# ------------------------------------------------------------ your judgement
#
# The two directories below are deliberately NOT ignored. Whether they should
# be depends on how large the library has grown and how private it is, so the
# rules are written out here, commented, rather than decided for you.


# state/ — transcripts and model output, keyed by fingerprint.
#
# Expensive: GPU-days of transcription and real money in model calls, and it is
# what makes rebuilding content/ cheap rather than starting from silence. Also
# large — hundreds of megabytes of machine-written JSON for a few thousand
# recordings — and nothing anybody reads.
#
#   Keep it in git   while the library is small, or if you would rather pay git
#                    than re-transcribe everything after a disk failure.
#   Ignore it        once it is past a few hundred megabytes. Then back it up
#                    some other way. Do not simply lose it.
#
# state/decisions/ stays in git either way: it is small, hand-written, and the
# record of what a person decided and why. That is exactly what history is for.
#
# /state/transcripts/
# /state/enrichment/


# content/ — the library, and the only thing that matters long-term.
#
# Small, textual, cheap to version, and its history is a record of what was
# decided about each recording. Committing it is most of the point of using git
# here at all.
#
# Ignore it only if the titles and write-ups are themselves the sensitive part
# and this repository is going somewhere public. A private remote is the better
# answer to that; ignoring content/ leaves you with a repository of READMEs.
#
# /content/


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- incidental

__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
.DS_Store
*.swp
*~
